        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">How motivational interviewing works</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">In this approach the psychologist does not tell you what to do. By talking openly about both sides of your ambivalence, we clarify what change would mean for you and what it would make possible. It is a short process and is often run alongside other therapeutic approaches.</p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Collaboration</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The relationship is a partnership in thinking, not an expert handing down advice.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Room for Ambivalence</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The part of you that wants to change and the part that doesn't are both heard; ambivalence is a natural stage, not a flaw.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Change Talk</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We notice and strengthen the motivation for change that already appears in your own words.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Autonomy and Choice</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The decision is always yours; choice rather than pressure is what makes change sustainable.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
